{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_n37qwkjapf7qvouvyqiebmi6orpoz55icdaahuwoetu5dut7atwa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Translation complete and verified.\n\n## What I did\n\n**Enumerated the full surface first.** Built the C library with CMake and ran `nm -D` on `libdriver.so`. The library is small (3 `.c` files + 3 headers, 658 lines) and exports exactly **9 public symbols**. Notably, `include/shared.h` defines three *non-`static`* functions **in the header**, so they leak into the `.so` as public symbols — easy to miss. No namespace-renaming macros exist, so linker names equal source names.\n\n**Nailed the ABI before writing code.** A C probe confirmed every offset I then hard-coded: `file_queue` = 440 bytes (`fp` @288, `f_status` @296), `alert_data` = 96 bytes, `struct stat` = 144 with `st_mtim` @88, `struct tm` = 56. These are `const{assert!}`-ed in the Rust so a layout regression can't compile.\n\nI bound directly to libc for allocation and stdio, so memory from `os_calloc`/`os_strdup` is `free()`-able by the caller and `FILE*` handles are interchangeable. String *scanning* is safe Rust over byte slices (index-based, out-of-range reads as NUL); only genuinely pointer-level operations stay unsafe.\n\n**Bugs preserved, not fixed:**\n- `al_data->filename[strlen(...) - 1] = '\\0'` underflows to a 1-byte write *before* the allocation when the name is empty — reproduced via `wrapping_offset`.\n- `p = str + 9` reads past the NUL into the previous line's leftovers, so my `str` buffer persists across loop iterations rather than being cleared.\n- `goto l_error` skips the trailing `feof()` check — modeled with an explicit flag, not just a fallthrough.\n- `log_size` is never incremented (the increment is commented out), so `log_size < LOG_LIMIT` is always true; kept as-is.\n- `atoi` is lowered by GCC to `strtol` (visible in the C object's relocations), so I call `strtol` to match overflow clamping.\n\n## Verification\n\n| Test | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Symbol gate (`nm -D` diff, both directions) | **empty** — 9/9 |\n| Hand-built edge cases (16 inputs) | 6288 B stdout + 794 B stderr **identical** |\n| Fuzz, 8 seeds × ~150 files | **identical** (~700 KB compared) |\n| Random bytes + mutated alerts (280 files) | 315 KB **identical**, 1119 alerts parsed |\n| `driver()` end-to-end, 630 calls | **identical** |\n| `CRALERT_FP_SET` with caller-supplied `fp` | **identical** — confirms `mon=[Jul]`, `name=[<stdin>]`, `last_change==st_mtime` |\n| `file_sleep`/timeout timing | **5s and 15s on both** |\n| `os_strdup(NULL)` fatal path | exit 1, identical stderr (no trailing newline) |\n\n`cargo build --release`: clean, **zero warnings**. `c_src/` unmodified (0 files touched).\n\nTwo things worth flagging: the `perror` outputs matched byte-for-byte *including* the errno-derived text (`\"date of location not NULL: Invalid argument\"`), which independently confirms errno state lines up at those call sites. And one deliberate deviation — a `tm_mon` outside `0..=11` indexes `s_month` out of bounds in the C, which is UB with no defined result; I skip the write instead. `mon` is never read back by any function and isn't part of any return value, so no observable output changes — but the C might crash there where mine won't. That's the only spot where I couldn't be bit-exact by construction."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1415,"cache_read_input_tokens":103943,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1415,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6e4995fe-72ff-4958-ae52-c7d78c60abe3","uuid":"47ecc6a2-9f5c-45f8-b253-a358ac063c70","timestamp":"2026-08-20T03:15:18.350Z"}
